Here is a simple implementation of the Bubble sort algorithm in Rust:
```
fn bubble_sort(arr: &mut [i32]) {
    let mut n = arr.len();
    while n > 1 {
        for i in 0..n-1 {
            for j in 0..n-i-1 {
                if arr[j] > arr[j+1] {
                    let temp = arr[j];
                    arr[j] = arr[j+1];
                    arr[j+1] = temp;
                }
            }
        }
        n = arr.len();
    }
}
```
This function takes a mutable reference to a slice of `i32` values and performs a bubble sort on the slice, using the "inside-out" approach to the bubble sort algorithm. The function will return the sorted slice.

Here is an example of how you could use this function:
```
let mut arr = [4, 5, 2, 6, 1];
bubble_sort(&mut arr);
println!("{:?}", arr); // prints [1, 2, 4, 5, 6]
```
Note that this is a very simple implementation of the bubble sort algorithm and may not be the most efficient algorithm for sorting large arrays. There are many other more efficient sorting algorithms available, such as quicksort and merge sort.
